<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The launch screen: what fills the window before Flutter has drawn anything.

  HAND-WRITTEN, AND IT HAS TO STAY THAT WAY. This was generated by
  flutter_native_splash, which paints a ONE PIXEL PNG stretched to fill the
  screen - that is how it does a solid colour, and it is also why it cannot do
  a gradient. The package and its config were removed when this was written;
  running it again would overwrite this file with that pixel.

  A SHAPE, NOT A BITMAP. A vector gradient is a few hundred bytes, is sharp on
  every density, and needs no asset regenerating when a colour changes - where
  a gradient PNG would need five densities and would band on large screens.

  ANDROID 12 AND UP SEE LESS OF THIS THAN YOU THINK. The system splash on
  API 31+ is drawn by the OS from windowSplashScreenBackground, which takes a
  COLOUR and not a drawable, so a gradient is not possible there at all. This
  drawable fills the window after that splash and before Flutter's first
  frame. values-v31/styles.xml carries the solid colour, picked from the
  middle of this gradient so the handover is not a jump.
-->
<layer-list xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      THE GRADIENT ITSELF, violet into indigo, on the diagonal.

      angle 315 runs top-left to bottom-right. Android only accepts multiples
      of 45 here, which is why it is not some more considered number.

      7C3AED is the app's own brand primary from AppColors, and the two ends are
      a lighter tint and a deeper shade of the same family - so the splash is
      the app's palette rather than a new one.

      THE ENDS ARE FURTHER APART THAN THEY LOOK THEY NEED TO BE. Android
      spreads the three stops evenly across the gradient's whole axis, and on
      a tall screen a diagonal axis is long - so a closer pair left the screen
      reading as flat violet with one blue corner. The travel has to be
      exaggerated to survive the projection.
    -->
    <item>
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <gradient
                android:type="linear"
                android:angle="315"
                android:startColor="#C084FC"
                android:centerColor="#7C3AED"
                android:endColor="#312E81" />
        </shape>
    </item>

    <!--
      A GLOW OVER THE TOP, which is the part that makes a gradient read as
      deliberate rather than as a default. Two flat stops look like a template;
      an off-centre light source gives it somewhere to come from.

      WHITE AT 25% ALPHA, NOT A LIGHTER PURPLE, so it lifts whatever is beneath
      it - which means the colours below can change without this needing to.

      gradientRadius in %p is API 21+, which is why this layer only exists in
      drawable-v21. The plain drawable/ copy is the same gradient without it.
    -->
    <item>
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <gradient
                android:type="radial"
                android:centerX="0.28"
                android:centerY="0.18"
                android:gradientRadius="90%p"
                android:startColor="#40FFFFFF"
                android:endColor="#00FFFFFF" />
        </shape>
    </item>

</layer-list>
